A||N||10||Chumby||Dash speakers, 85 SE||||5||
Z||000000||Chumby||12-08-1999||09:25 PM||||Howdie Fiero Fans.  I'm new to the chat group.  Sorry folks, this question won't be much of a challenge for you.  I want to know what size speakers go in the front dash of an 85 SE.  Surely they can't be 6x9.  Are they 5x7 or maybe even smaller?  The originals have finally blown and now they sound like a 1947 transitor radio.  Do I install replacements from underneath?<BR>Thanks  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Linx||12-08-1999||09:43 PM||||4"x10"...and Autozone carries 'em for $13 apiece.  Don't let anyone bluff you about the sound quality; their pretty good speakers.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Chumby||12-08-1999||09:55 PM||||I appreciate the tip, Linx.  One more question for now.  Like a dummy, I turned off and dropped the headlights last winter (I'm in Toronto, Canada) and a piece of ice got jammed in there and blew the headlight motor.  Is replacing the headlight motor quick and easy?  I think I may be able to grab one from a local wrecker.  Thx. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||12-08-1999||09:56 PM||||Radio Shack also has the 4x10 for $14.00.<BR>They sound good.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-08-1999||10:16 PM||||Chum, you better go look at the other speaker topic that is currently running right now, unless you want to stay with the same crappy sounding type of speaker as the originals. You do replace the front speakers from the top and they are 4" X 10". The grill covers just pop off.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-08-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||12-09-1999||07:03 AM||||The headlight motor is not too difficult to replace.<P>Unplug the wiring connector first to avoid any surprises.  I'f I recall there are four bolts (around 10mm) that hold the housing to the frame.  They will be mounted horizontally from the front of the housing.  It's easiest if you manually crank the light to the up position to gain access to these bolts.  Removing these bolts wlll allow the entire headlight assembly to be lifted out.<P>You might want to scribe some alignment marks on the frame first so you can get the assembly aligned properly when you put it back in.<P>Once the assembly is on the bench you'll see how it is put together.<P>Are you sure the motor is shot?  What does it do when you turn the lights on now?  Does it make any weird noises?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-09-1999||07:06 AM||||One more thing. When you get the assembly installed with the new working motor save the blue (I think) wire for last to hook up.  Once it is connected the motor will immediately retract the hedlight.  You don't want any body parts in the way when this happens.  Just to be on the safe side it is a good idea to disconnect the negative battery cable for this procedure.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voyd||12-09-1999||07:25 AM||||hey, was about to post a question on the stock dash speakers in a 85SE. whats the stock Brand?||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-09-1999||07:42 AM||||I looked at the factory speakers I replaced from my 85 SE and they say "Pontiac AMR--Delco GM" on them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ggarsk||12-09-1999||10:20 AM||||Another thing on the headlilight motors...you don't have to take the bottom bolts all the way out. The bottoms are slotted. It makes it much easier then trying to start bolts when you can't see the holes. Check out this site: <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm</A>   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||12-09-1999||11:00 AM||||I'm not a tailor but seems something got cross threaded||||reg||1
